    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>On an apartment complex, the claim is only as strong as the documentation for each building. Carriers sometimes inspect a few buildings, find limited damage on those, and apply the result to the whole property. With 10, 20, or 40 buildings, each with many roof facets, that sampling can leave a large part of the loss off the estimate. We inspect and document every building separately and keep the rent loss and code requirements in view from the start.</p>

<h2>Many buildings, many roof facets</h2>
<p>Garden-style complexes in Texas often have hip or cut-up gable roofs with a dozen or more slopes per building, plus breezeway roofs, carports, clubhouse, leasing office, laundry buildings, and pool structures. Hail and wind don't hit all of those evenly. Building orientation, tree cover, and roof age all change what you find on each slope.</p>
<p>That's why we label each building with the property's own building numbers and record:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Test squares and damage counts on each directional slope</li>
<li>Gutters, downspouts, vents, and flashings for that building</li>
<li>Siding, trim, windows, and screens on each elevation</li>
<li>HVAC condensers at ground level or on the roof</li>
<li>Carports, fences, signage, and amenity structures as separate items</li>
</ul>
<p>The Xactimate estimate follows the same building numbers. When the carrier says "we found no damage on Building 14," the answer is a photo set for Building 14, not a general disagreement.</p>

<h2>Deductibles and limits across buildings</h2>
<p>How the wind and hail deductible applies matters a great deal on a multi-building property. A percentage deductible may be calculated per building, per location, or per occurrence, depending on the wording. The same is true of limits: a blanket limit across the complex behaves differently from a scheduled limit on each building. Read the declarations and the statement of values together before deciding which buildings to include. Our <a href="/commercial-claims/">commercial claims overview</a> explains blanket limits and coinsurance in more detail.</p>

<h2>Water that moves between units</h2>
<p>In stacked apartments, a roof leak in a top-floor unit becomes a ceiling leak in the unit below, and a supply line failure on the third floor can reach the first. Water travels along floor framing and through plumbing chases, so the damaged area is often larger than the stains suggest. For each affected unit, we record moisture readings, the path the water took, and what was removed during mitigation.</p>
<p>Mitigation companies on apartment losses often work fast across many units. Their moisture logs, photos, and equipment records are part of your claim, so ask for them per unit. Mold that develops after a water loss may fall under a sublimit or exclusion, which makes prompt drying important. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> for more on documenting interior water losses.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Tenant displacement and loss of rents</h2>
<p>When units become uninhabitable, the owner loses rent. Many apartment policies cover this as loss of rents, rental value, or business income. The claim is built from:</p>
<ul>
<li>The rent roll before the loss, showing occupied units and rents</li>
<li>A list of units taken offline and the dates they came back</li>
<li>Lease terms on rent abatement</li>
<li>Concessions and lost renewals tied to the damage, where the policy allows them</li>
</ul>
<p>Residents' own belongings and their hotel costs are generally covered by their renters policies, not the owner's policy. Keeping that line clear in resident communications avoids confusion and complaints. If units stay offline because of a slow repair estimate, the length of the period of restoration becomes a negotiation point in itself.</p>

<h2>Code upgrades on older properties</h2>
<p>Many Texas apartment complexes are several decades old. When older buildings are repaired after a major loss, the local building department may require current-code work: fire separations between units, smoke alarms, electrical updates, energy-code insulation, accessibility changes, or roof deck and underlayment requirements. Those costs are often excluded from the base policy unless you carry ordinance or law coverage. We check for that endorsement early and ask the building department what it will require, because it changes the scope of every building being repaired.</p>

<h2>Working with property management</h2>
<p>On apartment claims, the people with the information are usually on-site managers and maintenance staff, while the insured may be an ownership entity in another city. We work through whoever the owner designates: we schedule access to units, get the work order history for leaks, and coordinate with the mitigation company and roofing contractor. Under Texas law we don't take part in the repairs ourselves. For roofs specifically, see our <a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">roof and hail damage claims</a> service.</p>
